<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Latin Archbishopric of Patras</h1><p class="st-meta">1276 CE – 1430 CE (approx.) · capital Patras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Church-ruled state in the north-western Peloponnese, where the Latin archbishops of Patras governed the city as secular lords for over 150 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> archbishop of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a> was the senior Catholic churchman of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>. Around 1276 the archbishops acquired the rights of the Barony of Patras, giving them lordship over the city and its land in addition to their spiritual role.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The archbishops governed with the help of canons and knights, while acting as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and increasingly as independent rulers under papal protection. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a> traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and currants with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. To defend themselves from the Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, the archbishops placed the city under Venetian administration from 1408 to 1413.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1429 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine Palaiologos</a>, future last Byzantine emperor, took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a> from Archbishop Pandolfo Malatesta's officials, and the citadel surrendered in 1430. Patras joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a> until the Ottoman conquest of 1458.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/latin-archbishopric-of-patras.jpg" alt="Latin Archbishopric of Patras" width="320" height="247"><figcaption>Image: Original design: unknown 13th-century artist; Sketch: Gustave Schlumberger (1844–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_John,_Latin_Archbishop_of_Patras_(Schlumberger,_1897).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1276 CE – 1430 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Patras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pandolfo Malatesta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Archbishopric of Patras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1353&amp;focus=latin-archbishopric-of-patras" aria-label="Latin Archbishopric of Patras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5221 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Latin Archbishopric of Patras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5541 1060L5541 1060M5541 1060L5530 1071M5541 1060L5293 831M5541 1060L5560 1098"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5541 1060h0M5530 1071h0M5293 831h0M5560 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5541 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5541 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1353&amp;focus=latin-archbishopric-of-patras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
